WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.984
Cloud incidents can involve virtual machines, containers, serverless functions, managed services, object storage, snapshots, firewall rules, security groups, routing, and data flows.

2
00:00:11.984 --> 00:00:16.971
The response approach depends on the affected service and evidence available.

3
00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:26.871
A compromised virtual machine offers different evidence and containment options than an exposed storage bucket or suspicious serverless function update.

4
00:00:26.871 --> 00:00:32.528
Workload response should preserve evidence before destructive actions when practical.

5
00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:44.959
Snapshotting disks, capturing metadata, recording configuration, preserving relevant logs, and documenting network controls can allow later analysis while containment and recovery proceed.

6
00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:58.209
Quarantine patterns may include restricting network access, moving a workload into an isolated security group, detaching risky permissions, or replacing the workload from trusted infrastructure-as-code.

7
00:00:58.209 --> 00:01:02.303
Storage incidents often involve exposure or unexpected access.

8
00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:09.895
Exposed object storage, public snapshots, broadly shared files, or unexpected data transfer require careful scoping.

9
00:01:09.895 --> 00:01:23.219
Responders need to identify what data was accessible, when exposure began, who or what accessed it, whether logs are available, and what business, privacy, legal, or customer notification questions may follow.

10
00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:33.566
Network incidents may appear as suspicious outbound traffic, unexpected firewall changes, exposed management ports, or unusual connectivity between services.

11
00:01:33.566 --> 00:01:38.925
Cloud network controls are highly programmable, so configuration history matters.

12
00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:45.401
A rule may have been changed manually, through automation, or as part of an infrastructure deployment.

13
00:01:45.401 --> 00:01:49.048
Understanding the change path helps prevent recurrence.

14
00:01:49.048 --> 00:01:55.078
Cloud-native containment should avoid destroying evidence or creating unnecessary outages.

15
00:01:55.078 --> 00:01:58.204
Sometimes isolation is better than termination.

16
00:01:58.204 --> 00:02:04.754
Sometimes rebuilding from known-good infrastructure is safer than trying to clean a running workload.

17
00:02:04.754 --> 00:02:14.059
The right choice depends on business criticality, evidence needs, data sensitivity, and the confidence responders have in the affected resource.
